From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] socket: non-blocking datagram sockets, drop what the host
 cannot take at once

What: UDP, ICMP and ICMPv6 sockets (and the listening TCP socket of a host
forward) are non-blocking from creation. A sendto() that the host cannot
take at once (EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK, or ENOBUFS from a full interface queue)
drops the datagram like a busy link would, instead of answering the guest
with ICMP "network unreachable": it is counted, and logged at most once a
minute with its destination ("dropped a datagram to A port P ..."). The
count is the last line of slirp_connection_info() (QEMU: "info usernet").
Receives that find nothing after all (EAGAIN) keep the socket.

Why (incident 2026-10-05 14:21, OmacVM 2.9.0 RC12 on a Mac mini, macOS 27):
the whole VM froze. sample(1) showed QEMU's main loop thread, holding the
BQL, in virtio_net_tx_bh -> net_slirp_receive -> udp_input -> sosendto ->
__sendto for the full sample; every vCPU thread waited for the BQL. libslirp
4.9.4 creates its UDP sockets blocking and sends with flags 0, so when macOS
cannot queue a datagram at once (send buffer space held back, e.g. by a
network extension content filter, which XNU's sosend counts against the
socket's send space) sendto() waits in sbwait() - with QEMU's main loop and
the BQL. Earlier stalls at 13:59 and ~14:05 the same day fit the same cause
(guest event links dropped and came back). TCP sockets were already
non-blocking (tcp_fconnect, tcp_connect); the listening socket of a host
forward was not, and accept() on it could wait the same way when a client
resets between poll and accept().

Checked, not changed: TCP sends (slirp_send) are on non-blocking sockets;
the DNS forwarder sends through sosendto (covered here); its host server
lookup (res_ninit) is local and cached for 1 s; guestfwd/exec paths
(fork_exec, open_unix) are not used by QEMU's -netdev user in OmacVM.

Upstream: not sent yet. Written against libslirp 4.9.4 after
libslirp-darwin-icmp-matching.patch, libslirp-ipv4-udp-translation.patch and
omacvm-libslirp-host-ports.patch; nothing in it depends on them.

Test: app/runtime/Tests/net/test-slirp-udp-stall.sh (run by the runtime
build): a real QEMU with -netdev user and a guest-side socket; a sendto()
stall injected with XNU's semantics (blocking socket: waits; non-blocking:
EAGAIN). Old runtime: QMP stops answering (hang). This one: QMP and slirp's
ping keep answering, the drops are counted and logged.

